  "id": "sod-shock-tube",
  "name": "Sod shock tube · ideal-gas Euler",
  "category": "Compressible benchmark",
  "description": "Left (rho,u,p)=(1,0,1), right (0.125,0,0.1); diaphragm x=0.5, gamma=1.4, HLLC/SSP-RK2, t=0.2.",
  "capabilities": [
    "Workbench",
    "TwoD",
    "Editable",
    "Compressible",
    "HLLC",
    "SSP-RK2",
    "Exact reference",
    "Unvalidated"
  ],
  "expectations": {
    "closed_domain": false,
    "positive_streamwise_flow": false,
    "curved_boundary": false,
    "non_ideal_mesh_quality": false,
    "notes": "G. A. Sod (1978), A survey of several finite difference methods for systems of nonlinear hyperbolic conservation laws. https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9991(78)90023-2 . Standard unit-length shock tube, scaled textbook units; inviscid ideal gas, gamma=1.4, R=1. A thin 2D mapped strip represents the 1D problem with slip transverse walls and transmissive ends. Explicit conservative mass, momentum and total energy; acoustic CFL=0.3; first-order spatial HLLC flux and SSP-RK2 in time. Exact Riemann profiles are overlaid at t<=0.2 only when saved inputs match the standard setup. Check density, absolute pressure, velocity, rarefaction, contact and shock; refine axial resolution. Run through the compressible controls in Solution, not SIMPLE. No reference-accuracy validation has been performed."
  },
